WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.234
-Our next guest is an Oscar
and Emmy-nominated actress

00:00:03.234 --> 00:00:04.701
you know from films such as
"Good Will Hunting"

00:00:04.701 --> 00:00:07.167
and shows like "Will &amp; Grace"
and "Speechless."

00:00:07.167 --> 00:00:09.267
Her new book,
"Managing Expectations:

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:11.200
A Memoir in Essays,"
is on sale today.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.300
Please welcome back to the show
Minnie Driver, everybody!

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:22.100
♪♪

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.968
♪♪

00:00:27.968 --> 00:00:29.834
Welcome back!
-Oh, thank you.

00:00:29.834 --> 00:00:31.868
-It's so lovely
to have you here.

00:00:31.868 --> 00:00:34.200
And this is maybe a little bit
different press

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:35.701
than you are used to doing.

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:38.100
You're not promoting
a TV show or a film.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:39.734
You're promoting a book.

00:00:39.734 --> 00:00:42.701
And is it true that
for a promotional event

00:00:42.701 --> 00:00:44.601
I believe yesterday...
-Yes?

00:00:44.601 --> 00:00:46.000
-...you were in a kayak?
-Yes.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.767
-Now, that is not something
that I would have predicted.

00:00:48.767 --> 00:00:51.100
-Well, I like kayaking.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.567
[ Laughter ]

00:00:52.567 --> 00:00:55.367
And it's not a normal thing
that one does in New York.

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:56.934
-No.

00:00:56.934 --> 00:00:58.801
You're the first I've met.

00:00:58.801 --> 00:00:59.968
-You know what? We --

00:00:59.968 --> 00:01:02.267
It was pouring
with rain yesterday,

00:01:02.267 --> 00:01:03.534
as you might have noticed.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:05.434
-It was, yeah.

00:01:05.434 --> 00:01:07.734
-I had the best time ever.

00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:09.501
-Which were you --
Was it the Hudson?

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:10.901
Where were you kayaking?
-Yeah, on the Hudson.

00:01:10.901 --> 00:01:11.934
-Okay.

00:01:11.934 --> 00:01:13.267
-And there was a guy
in another kayak

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.934
who was taking my picture
for this newspaper article.

00:01:15.934 --> 00:01:18.167
And, you know, there was a flash
because it was pitch black

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:20.667
because of the rain
and the high seas.

00:01:20.667 --> 00:01:24.100
And the Coast Guard came up
to us and they were like,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.367
"Are you alright?!
We thought you was signaling!"

00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:29.901
And it was due to, like,
the flash of the photography.

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:32.834
-I can't believe you stuck with
the kayak plan even in the rain.

00:01:32.834 --> 00:01:36.534
-I caught a wave from the wake
when the Coast Guard felt,

00:01:36.534 --> 00:01:38.100
you know, that we were fine.
-Yeah.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:40.634
-And they pulled away, like,
"Starsky &amp; Hutch" style,

00:01:40.634 --> 00:01:42.701
and there was, like, a massive
wave from their wake.

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:45.601
And I literally, like --
I kayaked down the wave.

00:01:45.601 --> 00:01:47.434
-Wow! This is very impressive.

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:49.367
I hope the photos
will be as exciting

00:01:49.367 --> 00:01:50.667
as you're making this sound.

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:52.267
-Well, it's a shame
there's no video because

00:01:52.267 --> 00:01:54.000
it's a little about the --
it's all about --

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.000
I don't know how to kayak.
Whatever. It was really --

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.667
-Yeah, I like that it does
seem like a nice time

00:01:58.667 --> 00:02:01.334
because, like, this young woman
here having the water wings.

00:02:01.334 --> 00:02:02.868
Like, who would have
ever guessed one day

00:02:02.868 --> 00:02:05.100
she'd be kayaking down
the Hudson River?

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:06.868
The rough waters of the Hudson.

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.234
-You totally would have known.

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:10.200
Like, they've retouched
the color on this

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.133
because it was a slate-gray day.

00:02:12.133 --> 00:02:14.634
It was probably 50 degrees,
freezing cold.

00:02:14.634 --> 00:02:16.701
That was at the beach
in England,

00:02:16.701 --> 00:02:18.334
which is not somewhere
you ever want to go.

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:21.000
-I like that every photo
of a beach in England

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.968
they have to color-correct.
-100 percent.

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:24.601
Because if they didn't,

00:02:24.601 --> 00:02:28.334
you'd be like, "Oh, my God!
That just looks awful!"

00:02:28.334 --> 00:02:30.467
Yeah.
-So this is a book --

00:02:30.467 --> 00:02:32.901
It's a memoir,
but it's personal essays.

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:34.734
You sort of tell it essay-style.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:38.100
And I imagine --

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.601
Obviously it's really personal.

00:02:39.601 --> 00:02:41.400
Do the people in your lives --
Have they read it?

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:44.234
I mean, it seems very honest.
-Yeah.

00:02:44.234 --> 00:02:47.267
I mean, I wrote --
It's dedicated to --

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:51.501
It's dedicated to my mum,
but also to my English teachers,

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:55.000
one of whom is this man
called Alastair Langlands,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.234
who features in the book.

00:02:56.234 --> 00:02:59.801
And I sent it to him,
and he liked it,

00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:01.467
so I feel like we're good to go.

00:03:01.467 --> 00:03:03.000
-That's great.
Is there anything

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:04.901
that didn't make the cut that,
looking back, you're like,

00:03:04.901 --> 00:03:06.901
"Oh, that was a good story,
that should have been in there"?

00:03:06.901 --> 00:03:08.701
-Yeah, there's
a really good story.

00:03:08.701 --> 00:03:11.033
So, the book
kind of got derailed.

00:03:11.033 --> 00:03:13.267
Like, my mom died in the middle
of me writing this book.

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.801
-Our condolences.
-Thank you.

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:17.934
And I didn't think
I could finish it,

00:03:17.934 --> 00:03:19.434
so I had to shorten the book,

00:03:19.434 --> 00:03:21.400
and the story
that didn't make it in that

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:24.367
was one of my mother's favorites
was when I made the movie

00:03:24.367 --> 00:03:26.400
"Phantom of the Opera,"

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.434
I sang the title track
to that movie,

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:29.968
and it got nominated
for an Academy Award,

00:03:29.968 --> 00:03:31.501
which was really exciting.

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:35.133
And I was in rehearsals to sing
the song at the Academy Awards,

00:03:35.133 --> 00:03:38.033
you know, like you do when
your song has been nominated.

00:03:38.033 --> 00:03:40.300
-Yeah.
-And I got a call one day

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.901
from the producer,
and they're like,

00:03:41.901 --> 00:03:43.801
"Yeah, you're not singing
the song anymore."

00:03:43.801 --> 00:03:45.334
And I was like,
"What do you mean, though?"

00:03:45.334 --> 00:03:47.534
"Yeah, Beyoncé
is singing your song."

00:03:47.534 --> 00:03:49.367
And I was like, "Wait. What?"

00:03:49.367 --> 00:03:51.367
"Beyoncé is singing
all the songs."

00:03:51.367 --> 00:03:54.200
And I was like, "I mean,
look, mate, I know it's Beyoncé

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.133
and everything
and she's like, Beyoncé,

00:03:57.133 --> 00:03:58.834
but, like, that is my song!

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:01.234
Like, what's happening?!"

00:04:01.234 --> 00:04:03.901
Anyway, I was so sad.

00:04:03.901 --> 00:04:09.000
I felt so incredibly sad about
this thing being taken away.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.300
My best mate, Emma Forrest,
was like,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.334
"The best thing we can do
is go on holiday,"

00:04:14.334 --> 00:04:16.667
which was a terrible idea.

00:04:16.667 --> 00:04:18.868
Anyway, we went on holiday
to Costa Rica.

00:04:18.868 --> 00:04:20.133
-That's a lovely place to go.

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:22.000
-Well, you'd think
if you go to Costa Rica.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.534
-Yeah.
-So we were -- It was late.

00:04:24.534 --> 00:04:26.400
We pulled our bags around
to the little tiny place

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.968
where you take
an internal flight.

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:30.000
They weigh you, they put you
on a plane, they give you a tag.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.734
20 minutes into the flight
when we're meant to be landing

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:34.434
by the sea,

00:04:34.434 --> 00:04:36.200
it's just jungle...

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.801
and it's more jungle
for another hour.

00:04:38.801 --> 00:04:42.200
And we land --
You ever had a Brazilian wax?

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.601
-Uh, no.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.601 --> 00:04:45.701
-Meaning to.

00:04:45.701 --> 00:04:47.300
[ Laughter ]

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:49.467
-Let us know when you do.

00:04:49.467 --> 00:04:51.100
If you can imagine the jungle

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:54.400
having had, like,
a Brazilian wax,

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.200
just for, like, the runway...

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:58.534
-Okay.
-That's where we landed.

00:04:58.534 --> 00:05:00.701
But it's not in Costa Rica.
It's in Panama.

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:02.501
-Now, at any point,
did they say --

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.334
Like, did the captain
make an announcement?

00:05:04.334 --> 00:05:05.701
-No!
-Okay.

00:05:05.701 --> 00:05:08.067
-There's no --
It's in this tiny rickety plane.

00:05:08.067 --> 00:05:10.300
-Did you, like,
buy discount tickets?

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.534
-No. They just gave us --
They just gave us the wrong --

00:05:13.534 --> 00:05:15.267
They give you, like,
a color-coded thing to get --

00:05:15.267 --> 00:05:17.501
-You got on the wrong plane?
-We just got on the wrong plane

00:05:17.501 --> 00:05:19.801
and we went to Panama,
and they dropped us off.

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:23.934
And I was begging the guy
to please let us get back

00:05:23.934 --> 00:05:25.567
on the plane
and go back to Costa Rica.

00:05:25.567 --> 00:05:27.000
And he was like,
"The plane's full."

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.934
Everybody else
was getting on the plane.

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:30.667
We were literally
in the middle of the jungle.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:32.567
And I was like,
"But when are you coming back?!"

00:05:32.567 --> 00:05:34.701
And he was like,
"I'll be back in a week."

00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:36.701
And I was like, "No, no, wait!

00:05:36.701 --> 00:05:38.601
Oh, don't leave us!"
And they left us.

00:05:38.601 --> 00:05:40.901
And it's like a terrible film

00:05:40.901 --> 00:05:42.834
of me and my friend
with our wheelie bags

00:05:42.834 --> 00:05:46.234
and our, like, wedgie heels
ready for Costa Rica

00:05:46.234 --> 00:05:48.267
in the middle of the jungle
down a dirt track.

00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:50.767
And I'm like,
"We're definitely gonna die!"

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:53.868
And my best friend was like,
"Yes, we are!"

00:05:53.868 --> 00:05:55.367
-It's just funny to think of you

00:05:55.367 --> 00:05:58.033
at a, like, tiny airport
in the jungle being like,

00:05:58.033 --> 00:06:00.601
"I was supposed to sing
at the Oscars!"

00:06:00.601 --> 00:06:03.234
-I was -- scream --
Like, screaming to the monkeys,

00:06:03.234 --> 00:06:05.033
"I was supposed to sing
in the Oscars!"

00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:07.334
-And they're saying like,
"That lady keeps telling us

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:08.767
she got replaced by Beyoncé.

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:11.968
She's --
There's a very strange --

00:06:11.968 --> 00:06:15.734
There's a very strange woman out
on the Brazilian wax runway."

00:06:15.734 --> 00:06:19.434
-"She's crazy. Take her to
Panama and leave her there!"

00:06:19.434 --> 00:06:22.901
-So, your son is 12.

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:24.567
Is he curious as a 12-year-old,

00:06:24.567 --> 00:06:27.834
curious when his mother
writes a memoir?

00:06:27.834 --> 00:06:32.167
-I mean, he really --
He is so not interested

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:33.767
in anything I d--

00:06:33.767 --> 00:06:38.167
I mean, he's so lovely.
I did rope him into --

00:06:38.167 --> 00:06:40.133
I have a podcast called
"Minnie Questions,"

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:43.601
and I ask the same seven
questions to everybody.

00:06:43.601 --> 00:06:45.968
And for this Mother's Day
coming,

00:06:45.968 --> 00:06:49.067
I asked Henry
if he would interview me.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:51.200
And he was so nice about it,

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.767
and he was like,
"Yeah, no, I will."

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.200
And I was like,
"And you know..."

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.400
He really does have a deep voice

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:56.901
and he sounds like
he's a bookie.

00:06:56.901 --> 00:06:59.934
And I was like, "So will you --
You know that when you ask

00:06:59.934 --> 00:07:02.200
the questions, like,
ask me the questions,

00:07:02.200 --> 00:07:04.934
but then perhaps there should
be, like, follow-up questions."

00:07:04.934 --> 00:07:07.334
He's like, "No, I got it.
I got it. I listen. It's okay."

00:07:07.334 --> 00:07:11.701
So it's like, "So, Mom, so,
what would your last meal be?"

00:07:11.701 --> 00:07:13.701
And I, like, go through
the last meal and I'm like,

00:07:13.701 --> 00:07:17.734
"You know,
and then I'd have ice cream."

00:07:17.734 --> 00:07:20.234
"To follow up...

00:07:20.234 --> 00:07:23.200
what flavor ice cream
would you have

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:26.501
and would you also
add sprinkles?"

00:07:26.501 --> 00:07:28.701
And I was like, "Well..."
And he just did it.

00:07:28.701 --> 00:07:30.734
He did such a good job.
Like, he really --

00:07:30.734 --> 00:07:34.100
-I would be so proud.
That is absolutely excellent.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:35.133
-Ridiculously proud.

00:07:35.133 --> 00:07:37.234
He's such --
He's an interested,

00:07:37.234 --> 00:07:39.234
kind person who gets roped

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.634
into all kinds of things
that I ask him to do.

00:07:41.634 --> 00:07:43.601
And he could say no,
but he doesn't.

00:07:43.601 --> 00:07:45.868
-Curiosity from a kid
is the best thing in the world.

00:07:45.868 --> 00:07:47.734
When kids are genuinely
curious, it's the best.

00:07:47.734 --> 00:07:49.534
-I agree with you,
and it means that there is

00:07:49.534 --> 00:07:52.000
some level of relevancy
that you have as their parent...

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:53.501
-Yeah.
-...left.

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:55.901
-Do you feel like you're
holding on for dear life?

00:07:55.901 --> 00:07:58.400
-Hanging by a thread.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:01.234
-Well, at least you got
that one last podcast in.

00:08:01.234 --> 00:08:02.734
[ Laughter ]
-We'll always have that.

00:08:02.734 --> 00:08:04.267
-You'll always --
You can listen to it

00:08:04.267 --> 00:08:05.934
when you're like,
"Well, he hasn't called.

00:08:05.934 --> 00:08:07.701
I'll listen to that
ice-cream follow-up."

00:08:07.701 --> 00:08:09.901
-"He loved me once! He did!"
-Hey, thanks for being here.

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:12.901
Congratulations on the book.
You guys, Minnie Driver!

00:08:12.901 --> 00:08:14.334
"Managing Expectations"

00:08:14.334 --> 00:08:16.100
is on sale now
wherever you buy your books,

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:18.200
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.133
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

